WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.120
The painting you are looking at was created&nbsp;
more than five centuries ago by Leonardo da&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:11.520
Vinci. But just a few years ago, a detail was&nbsp;
discovered that had remained hidden all this time.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:17.600
Art historian Kelly Grovier published an article&nbsp;
on the BBC revealing a small detail that would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:23.520
expose one of the most subversive messages&nbsp;
Leonardo ever hid in his entire body of work.

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:26.240
This detail is located just above the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.720
hand of the Virgin Mary. A small&nbsp;
plant with a very peculiar shape,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.440
which Leonardo painted with a purpose very&nbsp;
different from what you might imagine.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.960
This work is called The Virgin of&nbsp;
the Rocks. And the first thing we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:42.640
need to clarify is that there&nbsp;
is not one, but two versions.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:47.440
The first was painted by Leonardo&nbsp;
da Vinci around 1483 and is now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:52.240
held at the Louvre in Paris. While&nbsp;
the second was started in 1495,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:57.200
completed thirteen years later, and is&nbsp;
now at the National Gallery in London.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.000
In both paintings we see the same&nbsp;
scene. The Virgin Mary is at the center,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:07.200
kneeling in a rocky grotto. To her right,&nbsp;
the child John the Baptist, and to her left,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:13.760
the archangel Uriel holding the infant Jesus. The&nbsp;
four figures form a perfect pyramid, surrounded by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:20.080
imposing rock formations, and beside them, we&nbsp;
find a calm pond that separates us from them.

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:25.520
Curiously, this scene does not appear in any&nbsp;
passage of the Bible. The story actually comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:31.600
from an apocryphal tradition that imagined&nbsp;
Jesus and John meeting by chance as children,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.720
while their families were fleeing the Massacre&nbsp;
of the Innocents ordered by King Herod.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:40.800
But what is truly strange about this&nbsp;
painting is not the scene it depicts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.320
but the place where Leonardo chose to set it.

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:49.280
In 1483, the Confraternity of the&nbsp;
Immaculate Conception of Milan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.760
commissioned Leonardo to paint the&nbsp;
central panel of their altarpiece.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:59.840
What they expected Leonardo to paint was a&nbsp;
traditional image. The Virgin and Child enthroned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.840 --> 00:02:05.920
surrounded by a choir of angels, celebrating the&nbsp;
doctrine that gave the confraternity its name:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:09.840
that Mary, like Christ, was conceived without sin.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:14.240
However, what Leonardo delivered&nbsp;
was something completely different.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:20.080
Leonardo painted a gloomy, desolate grotto.&nbsp;
A landscape of rocks sculpted by time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:26.240
so overwhelming that the sacred figures almost&nbsp;
seem secondary. But the most puzzling thing of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.240 --> 00:02:31.520
all is that in the work we see no evident&nbsp;
allusion to the Immaculate Conception.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:36.560
Historians have spent centuries wondering&nbsp;
why Leonardo made that decision. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:41.360
answer could lie in something that happened&nbsp;
to him shortly before he began to paint.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:46.320
Leonardo recorded in his notebooks an&nbsp;
episode that marked him profoundly.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:52.080
During an excursion through the mountains, while&nbsp;
wandering among shadowy rocks, he came across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:57.840
entrance to a great cavern. At that moment he&nbsp;
stopped in awe, and according to his own words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:04.000
he felt two contradictory emotions at the same&nbsp;
time: on one hand, fear of the threatening dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:09.440
cave, but at the same time, the desire to&nbsp;
see if there was something marvelous inside.

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.000
Leonardo finally decided to enter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.240
and what he found inside changed&nbsp;
his way of understanding the world.

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:21.360
Inside this cavern, Leonardo found a&nbsp;
fossilized whale and a great number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:26.080
ancient seashells embedded in the rock,&nbsp;
hundreds of kilometers from the sea.

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:31.440
For Leonardo, this was a great mystery.&nbsp;
From that moment on, he could not stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:36.000
asking himself how marine creatures had&nbsp;
reached the peaks of those mountains. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:41.680
considering the official explanation of the&nbsp;
Church, his questions became even deeper.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:47.360
For the Church, the Great Flood described in the&nbsp;
Old Testament was the answer to why those marine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:52.720
creatures were found there. According to the&nbsp;
biblical passage, the waters rose to such a point&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.720 --> 00:03:58.080
that they dragged the shells up to the summits,&nbsp;
and when they receded, they left them there.

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:02.480
But as you might expect, that&nbsp;
explanation did not convince Leonardo.

00:04:02.480 --> 00:04:06.720
We even know that the subject haunted&nbsp;
him just before starting this painting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:11.520
In his notebooks he recorded that in&nbsp;
1482, while working on a statue for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:16.240
the Duke of Milan, some peasants brought&nbsp;
a sack full of shells to his workshop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:20.560
found in the mountains of Parma and&nbsp;
Piacenza. And as he observed them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.560 --> 00:04:26.320
Leonardo came to the conclusion that those&nbsp;
creatures had not been dragged there by any flood.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:30.720
For Leonardo, those shells had been born&nbsp;
there. Which meant that those mountains,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:34.560
at some remote point in the past,&nbsp;
had been the bottom of the sea. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:39.120
that was only possible if the Earth was&nbsp;
far older than what the Church taught.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.240
We must keep in mind that&nbsp;
at this moment in history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:47.840
this was a heretical idea. Even speaking about&nbsp;
it in public could be considered dangerous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:51.440
so Leonardo decided to hide&nbsp;
it in one of his paintings.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:55.840
And this is where we arrive at the&nbsp;
detail I told you about at the beginning.

00:04:55.840 --> 00:05:00.400
Just above Mary's right hand, there is a&nbsp;
palm tree. At first glance it seems like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:06.000
just another element of the vegetation. It even&nbsp;
has a perfectly acceptable religious reading,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:10.160
since palms prefigure the ones that&nbsp;
would be thrown before Christ at his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:14.160
entrance into Jerusalem, the&nbsp;
Sunday before his crucifixion.

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:17.200
But let us look at that palm tree more closely.

00:05:17.200 --> 00:05:22.400
Its leaves, spread out like a fan, especially&nbsp;
sharp in this first version at the Louvre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.400 --> 00:05:27.280
imitate with astonishing precision&nbsp;
the contours of an open scallop shell.

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:32.080
And that cannot be a coincidence. In&nbsp;
Leonardo's notebooks we constantly see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:37.360
how one image transforms into another, as for&nbsp;
example in this drawing, where the spiral of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:43.200
a nautilus shell ends up becoming a woman's&nbsp;
hairstyle. Leonardo's imagination was capable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:48.720
of finding correspondences where no one else&nbsp;
could see them, so a palm tree whose leaves are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:54.400
identical to the ridges of a scallop is exactly&nbsp;
the kind of double image Leonardo mastered.

00:05:54.400 --> 00:05:58.160
But if this still does not convince&nbsp;
you, there is something more.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:03.680
The scallop was not just any symbol. It was&nbsp;
an emblem directly associated with Mary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:08.800
and specifically with the doctrine of her&nbsp;
Immaculate Conception. A decade earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:14.400
Piero della Francesca had painted the Virgin&nbsp;
beneath a shell-shaped dome, from which hung a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:20.240
pearl-shaped egg. Because according to the belief&nbsp;
of the time, pearls were born supernaturally from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:26.240
a drop of the purest dew. A miraculous and&nbsp;
immaculate conception, just like Mary's.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:29.840
And if you are wondering where the&nbsp;
pearl is in The Virgin of the Rocks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.840 --> 00:06:33.760
the answer is that Leonardo&nbsp;
did not paint one, but twenty.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:39.040
At the exact center of both versions, there&nbsp;
is a brooch holding Mary's mantle in place.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:43.280
And surrounding the central stone&nbsp;
of that brooch is a halo of twenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:47.840
dazzling pearls. If you follow the&nbsp;
trajectory of the hem of her mantle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:53.440
you will see that it guides your gaze directly&nbsp;
from those pearls to the scallop-shaped palm tree.

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:58.720
If we take all of this into account, it&nbsp;
is easy to find a connection. The shell,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:04.880
the pearls, the Immaculate Conception. Leonardo&nbsp;
did include the confraternity's doctrine in his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:10.720
painting. He just did it his own way. Fusing it&nbsp;
with the symbol of his most dangerous discovery:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:16.640
the seashells in the mountains that proved the&nbsp;
Church was wrong about the creation of the world.

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:22.880
And finally, there is one last detail that&nbsp;
could confirm that none of this was accidental.

00:07:22.880 --> 00:07:27.120
But before telling you what it is, I would&nbsp;
like to remind you that if you would like to&nbsp;&nbsp;

