your order once you finish watching the video.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:58.960
Now, when Leonardo painted the&nbsp;
second version, years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.960 --> 00:08:04.720
we can see that he changed every type of plant&nbsp;
that appeared in the first one. Every flower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:10.320
every leaf, and every species was replaced&nbsp;
with a different one. Except the palm tree.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:14.560
Leonardo not only kept it, but made&nbsp;
it look even more like a scallop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:18.720
Its leaves, more stylized in the&nbsp;
London version, resemble even more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:23.040
closely the striated grooves that&nbsp;
radiate from the hinge of a shell.

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:26.880
Of all the plant elements in the&nbsp;
painting, the only one Leonardo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:31.760
considered untouchable was precisely&nbsp;
the one that could hide his secret.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:35.200
Unfortunately, we will never&nbsp;
know if Leonardo did this with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:39.520
that intention. But after hearing all&nbsp;
the arguments, it is quite plausible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:43.920
that Leonardo wanted to include that&nbsp;
plant with a second purpose in mind.

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:47.440
As always, I would love for you to&nbsp;
leave me in the comments whether you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:50.400
believe this painting truly&nbsp;
holds a double intention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:55.760
or if on the contrary, it is nothing more&nbsp;
than a decorative element of the painting.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:08:58.480
I will be reading your opinions in the comments.

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:05.760
Thank you for watching the video until the&nbsp;
